AWS Deployment Guide — IAM User Setup → ECR Push (via Docker Compose)
Prerequisites
AWS CLI installed (aws --version to check)
Docker + Docker Compose installed
Root or admin access to the AWS account (to create the IAM user)
Step 1: Create a Separate IAM User (as root/admin)
Log in to the AWS Console with the root/admin account.
Go to IAM → Users → Create user.
Give it a name (e.g. deploy-user).
Console access — only enable this if a human needs to log into the AWS Console with this user. If this user is purely for CLI/Docker automation, skip it entirely (no console access needed).
If you do enable it: AWS will let you set an auto-generated or custom password, and you can require the user to reset it on first login. There's no automatic emailed "login link" for a standard IAM user — you manually share the console sign-in URL (https://<account-id>.signin.aws.amazon.com/console) and the password with them.
Permissions — attach only what's needed. For this workflow, at minimum:
AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryFullAccess (ECR push/pull, repo management)
AmazonECS_FullAccess (if you'll deploy to ECS later)
For production, replace these AWS-managed policies with a custom least-privilege policy scoped to the specific repos/cluster — full access is broader than most deployment users need.
Create the user.
Step 2: Create an Access Key for This User

Access keys are what let this user authenticate via CLI/SDK (separate from any console password).

IAM → Users → select the user → Security credentials tab
Under Access keys, click Create access key.
Choose use case: Command Line Interface (CLI).
AWS shows you the Access Key ID and Secret Access Key — copy both now. The secret is shown only once; you cannot retrieve it again later (only regenerate a new key).
Step 3: Configure AWS CLI with This User's Credentials (on your laptop)
powershell
aws configure

It will prompt for:

AWS Access Key ID → paste from Step 2
AWS Secret Access Key → paste from Step 2
Default region name → e.g. ap-south-1
Default output format → json (or leave blank)

Note: This is the Access Key ID/Secret, not the console username/password from Step 1 — those are two different credential types for two different login methods (console vs. programmatic).

Verify it worked:

powershell
aws sts get-caller-identity

This should return the ARN of the IAM user you just configured — confirming the CLI is authenticated as that user, not root.

Step 4: Create the ECR Repositories
powershell
aws ecr create-repository --repository-name my-backend
aws ecr create-repository --repository-name my-frontend
Step 5: Authenticate Docker with ECR
powershell
aws ecr get-login-password --region <YOUR_REGION> | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin <YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.<YOUR_REGION>.amazonaws.com

This token expires after 12 hours — re-run it whenever you get an auth error on push.

Step 6: Update docker-compose.yaml

Point image: at your ECR repo URLs (keep build: too — Compose will build locally and tag it as this image name):

yaml
networks:
  micro-networks:
    driver: bridge
volumes:
  backend-node-modules:
  frontend-node-modules:
  mongo-data:
  redis-data:
services:
  backend:
    build: ./backend
    image: <YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.<YOUR_REGION>.amazonaws.com/my-backend:latest
    networks:
      - micro-networks
    ports:
      - "3000:3000"
    volumes:
      - ./backend:/app
      - backend-node-modules:/app/node_modules
    environment:
      - MONGO_URI=mongodb://mongodb:27017/mydb
      - REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379
    depends_on:
      - mongodb
      - redis

  frontend:
    build: ./frontend
    image: <YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.<YOUR_REGION>.amazonaws.com/my-frontend:latest
    networks:
      - micro-networks
    ports:
      - "5173:5173"
    volumes:
      - ./frontend:/app
      - frontend-node-modules:/app/node_modules

  mongodb:
    image: mongo:latest
    container_name: mongodb
    restart: always
    networks:
      - micro-networks
    ports:
      - "27017:27017"
    volumes:
      - mongo-data:/data/db

  redis:
    image: redis:alpine
    container_name: redis
    restart: always
    networks:
      - micro-networks
    ports:
      - "6379:6379"
    volumes:
      - redis-data:/data
Step 7: Build the Images
powershell
docker compose build
Step 8: Push to ECR
powershell
docker compose push

This pushes my-backend and my-frontend to their respective ECR repos. mongodb and redis won't be pushed since they don't have a custom image: pointing to your ECR account (they're pulled from Docker Hub directly).
